ngful chance of being a terrorist. The pattern is a property of the small violent subset, not the vast peaceful whole. ### Why the pattern exists doesn't change the risk calculation You've argued that this pattern can't just be coincidence or geopolitics—that there's something inherent to the religion that produces it. Even if we granted that fully, it still wouldn't change the math. Imagine a religion that genuinely taught violence, and 0.5% of its followers acted on it (ten times higher than reality). That still means 99.5% don't. Your sister's flatmate would still be overwhelmingly likely to be peaceful. Pre-judging him based on the 0.5% would still be irrational and unfair. ### So, to your core point You're making a different claim than "most are terrorists," and you're right that I've been answering the wrong one. The pattern you're pointing to is real. But the pattern is about who commits terrorism globally, not about who your sister is sharing a kitchen with. Those are two completely different questions. The leap from "this group is overrepresented in a tiny, horrible phenomenon" to "therefore, a random member of this group is dangerous" is the same logical error as fearing male flatmates because most serial killers are men. Your sister's safety depends on her ability to assess the individual in front of her. The global pattern doesn't help her do that. It only loads the encounter with fear that the numbers don't justify. That's not me being preachy. That's just math.
